  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes design and fabrication strategies for curved sensors. At the top, it compares two common stretchable layouts used to accommodate curvature: an “origami” pattern made of petal-like hinged segments, and a “serpentine” mesh in which small square islands are linked by wavy interconnects. Both geometries visually suggest ways a flat device can deform without breaking when wrapped onto nonplanar surfaces.\n\nThe lower part focuses on how these sensor arrays are transferred or formed onto domed and bowl-shaped substrates. One approach, elastomer-assisted moulding, uses pre-stretched elastomeric materials so that a planar sensor mesh can be expanded over a curved shape or conformally transferred by imprinting. Another, mechanically assisted moulding, shows gas or liquid injection bulging a flexible substrate upward into a dome, or shaping it downward into a concave form while carrying the sensor pattern. A final route, direct material growth, appears to build the curved device directly on porous alumina membranes.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that curved sensors can be achieved either by using deformable circuit geometries or by moulding and growth methods that convert initially planar sensor structures into conformal 3D surfaces.",
